<!-- author: beefviper -->
<!-- date: Sept 30, 2026 -->

<!--
  Freeway, after the 1981 Atari 2600 game: two chickens, side by side at the
  bottom, each trying to get across ten lanes of traffic to the strip along the
  top. Each press of the up key is one hop, one lane; a chicken that is hit is
  put back at the start (and keeps what it has scored). The first to
  `crossings` trips across wins.

  On a grid of 50 pixel cells, 12 rows down:

    row  0        the far side, where a crossing scores (and the scores live)
    rows 1-10     ten lanes of traffic, alternating cars and trucks, each lane
                  with its own speed and direction
    row  11       the near side, where both chickens start

  It needs no verb the language did not have. It is Frogger's road without the
  river: <hop> for the chicken, a <group> per lane with <wrap /> so traffic
  loops, <reset /> for being hit, and <inc /> plus an <atleast> condition for
  scoring. Player 1 is W and S, player 2 is the up and down arrows.
-->

